1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,086
This is OneDecision, an agent built with Strands Agents, running on Claude Opus 5

2
00:00:05,086 --> 00:00:06,530
through Amazon Bedrock.

3
00:00:06,530 --> 00:00:11,145
It is for anyone who runs operations, where the same call lands on the same desk

4
00:00:11,145 --> 00:00:11,780
every week.

5
00:00:11,780 --> 00:00:14,590
Dana runs returns for a camera rental company.

6
00:00:14,590 --> 00:00:19,623
Twenty times a week, a kit comes back missing something small, and someone asks her

7
00:00:19,623 --> 00:00:20,290
what to do.

8
00:00:20,290 --> 00:00:24,650
She answers in thirty seconds, and she has never had an hour to write it down.

9
00:00:24,650 --> 00:00:28,360
Rule-based automation can't help, because nobody wrote the rule.

10
00:00:28,360 --> 00:00:31,220
Everything you'll see runs on synthetic data.

11
00:00:31,820 --> 00:00:36,540
A kit is checked in at the returns dock, and that event is what starts the work, not

12
00:00:36,540 --> 00:00:38,170
somebody typing at a chatbot.

13
00:00:38,170 --> 00:00:41,840
The Strands agent investigates with four read-only tool calls.

14
00:00:41,840 --> 00:00:46,734
It pulls the case, reconciles what arrived against the bill of materials, prices the

15
00:00:46,734 --> 00:00:49,530
missing part, and checks for an approved policy.

16
00:00:49,530 --> 00:00:52,500
Every line of evidence names the tool that produced it.

17
00:00:52,500 --> 00:00:55,700
One neoprene strap missing, fourteen dollars.

18
00:00:55,700 --> 00:01:00,490
The serial matches and there's no damage, but nothing on file covers a case like

19
00:01:00,490 --> 00:01:05,520
this, so the agent stops and asks once, then proposes where the boundary should sit.

20
00:01:06,120 --> 00:01:07,680
Dana isn't at her desk.

21
00:01:07,680 --> 00:01:12,018
She approves from her phone, between two other jobs, and that's the whole

22
00:01:12,018 --> 00:01:12,790
interruption.

23
00:01:13,390 --> 00:01:16,410
Her approval is recorded, and that is all it does.

24
00:01:16,410 --> 00:01:21,019
The agent now proposes a policy, and it can only propose in a constrained language,

25
00:01:21,019 --> 00:01:24,740
where every condition has to come from an allowlist of nine fields.

26
00:01:24,740 --> 00:01:29,784
The action set is fixed by the system, not by the agent: holding for parts, raising

27
00:01:29,784 --> 00:01:34,160
a replacement work order under the spend cap, and closing the exception.

28
00:01:34,160 --> 00:01:38,735
Then deterministic code replays the candidate against twenty-four historical cases,

29
00:01:38,735 --> 00:01:41,160
and it would have gotten zero of them wrong.

30
00:01:41,160 --> 00:01:45,480
If that number were anything but zero, the activate button wouldn't be here.

31
00:01:46,080 --> 00:01:50,440
A human activates it, and that's the only path from candidate to active.

32
00:01:50,440 --> 00:01:53,970
It takes a token, and it's refused without a passing replay.

33
00:01:53,970 --> 00:01:57,820
The model can't do it, because it isn't a tool the agent has.

34
00:01:58,420 --> 00:02:01,150
Another kit comes in, missing a body cap.

35
00:02:01,150 --> 00:02:05,220
Same investigation, but this time an approved policy matches.

36
00:02:05,220 --> 00:02:10,059
The work order is raised, the disposition is set, both writes are read back and

37
00:02:10,059 --> 00:02:14,040
verified, and the exception is closed against policy version one.

38
00:02:14,040 --> 00:02:16,080
Dana never saw this case.

39
00:02:16,680 --> 00:02:21,730
Now one that looks almost identical: same kit, one cheap accessory missing.

40
00:02:21,730 --> 00:02:26,820
But the serial doesn't match, so the wrong body came back, and the system refuses.

41
00:02:26,820 --> 00:02:31,800
A hard guardrail runs before any policy is consulted, and a policy can only ever

42
00:02:31,800 --> 00:02:33,170
narrow what's allowed.

43
00:02:33,170 --> 00:02:36,650
When the rules run out, the answer is to ask a person.

44
00:02:37,250 --> 00:02:41,140
The same agent is deployed to Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Runtime.

45
00:02:41,140 --> 00:02:46,202
An invocation from AWS runs the same four tools on Bedrock and returns the same

46
00:02:46,202 --> 00:02:50,110
decision card, with nothing executed until a person approves.

47
00:02:50,710 --> 00:02:55,090
The dashboard counts everything this demo just did, from the product's own records.

48
00:02:55,090 --> 00:03:00,555
Cases by outcome, the share handled automatically, the escalations, every agent run

49
00:03:00,555 --> 00:03:03,650
and tool call, and the tokens Bedrock reported.

50
00:03:04,250 --> 00:03:08,690
Every event, tool call, approval, and action lands in one log.

51
00:03:08,690 --> 00:03:13,662
It only ever appends, each entry is hashed onto the one before it, and the database

52
00:03:13,662 --> 00:03:14,800
refuses to edit it.

53
00:03:14,800 --> 00:03:19,967
Across twenty-four evaluation cases, one hundred percent correct escalation, and

54
00:03:19,967 --> 00:03:21,840
zero false automatic actions.

55
00:03:21,840 --> 00:03:23,990
An agent that can act is easy.

56
00:03:23,990 --> 00:03:27,400
An agent someone will let act unattended is the one that ships.

57
00:03:27,400 --> 00:03:28,880
Teach the agent once.

58
00:03:28,880 --> 00:03:31,800
It safely handles the next hundred decisions.
